course: religion-ethics
lesson: 90
title: Saying sorry and making amends: Zacchaeus pays back
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Saying sorry starts putting things right; making amends finishes it, by fixing, replacing or paying back what you damaged.
explain: Zacchaeus, the chief tax collector of Jericho, climbed a sycamore-fig tree to see Jesus; after Jesus came to his house, he gave half his possessions to the poor and paid back four times anyone he had cheated (Luke 19:1-10).
explain: A real apology names what you did, says sorry, shows you understand the hurt, offers to fix it and promises to change; "soz" in a text or a sorry muttered at your shoes is only the start.
explain: Christians believe God forgives completely, removing sins "as far as the east is from the west" (Psalm 103:12), but they also believe in putting things right with the people you hurt.
explain: Sorry also means trying to change, and the hurt person may need time before they can forgive, which is honest, not unkind.
fact: Zacchaeus gave half his possessions to the poor and paid back four times anyone he had cheated (Luke 19:1-10); the law asked only for the amount plus a fifth (Numbers 5:7).
fact: "Rend your heart and not your garments" (Joel 2:13): in Bible times sad people tore (rent) their clothes to show sorrow, but God wants sorrow inside, not only words or torn clothes.
fact: An apology is a meeting, not a message: Jesus said to go and be reconciled first (Matthew 5:23-24).
fact: A good apology names what you did, says sorry, shows you understand the hurt, offers to fix it and promises to change.
fact: Joseph's brothers sold him as a slave and fooled their father with Joseph's coat dipped in goat's blood (Genesis 37:31-33); years later, when they met him again in Egypt without knowing him, they said, "Surely we are being punished because of our brother" (Genesis 42:21).
fact: Joseph forgave them: "You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good" (Genesis 50:20).
fact: Scripture asks for both: confess the wrong and make it good (Numbers 5:6-7); a new pen without a sorry is only half.
fact: The New Testament word for repentance, metanoia, means a change of mind and direction; a sorry repeated every day without change is not real.
fact: Grown-ups should say sorry to children too: "all have sinned" (Romans 3:23).
fact: Catholics and Orthodox confess to a priest, who speaks God's forgiveness and must never tell anyone (the seal of confession); all Christians confess to God: "If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just and will forgive us" (1 John 1:9).
fact: Restorative justice asks: who was hurt, what do they need, and whose job is it to put it right?
fact: Christians are called to forgive as they are forgiven (Matthew 6:12; Colossians 3:13), but forgiveness can be a journey and does not mean pretending you are not hurt.
fact: Putting right an old wrong still matters, especially when someone else took the blame (Exodus 20:16; Luke 19:8).
fact: Slang check: "soz" means sorry; "k" in a text means OK, often a cold OK; "wee" (Belfast, Scotland) means little; "sweet as" (New Zealand) means fine.
exercise: What did Zacchaeus do after meeting Jesus? | Gave half his things to the poor and paid back four times anyone he cheated | Climbed down and ran away from Jesus | Became a tax collector for the first time | Paid back exactly what he took and nothing more
exercise: Why did Zacchaeus give back four times, not just what he took? | Real sorrow wants to fix things generously, not do the minimum | Because the law ordered all tax collectors to pay back ten times | Because Jesus told him the exact amount to pay | Because he wanted everyone to think he was rich
exercise: Kian mutters "sorry" at his shoes because the teacher made him. What does Joel 2:13 say God wants? | Sorrow inside, not only words | Only the right words, said loudly | Nothing at all once you've said it | Torn clothes to show you're sad
exercise: Lexi texts "soz" after missing her friend's party. Her friend replies "k". What would be better? | Saying it face to face: "I'm really sorry I missed your party" | Sending "soz" again, this time with lots more emojis and a heart | Saying nothing until the friend forgets | Texting "k" back to be fair
exercise: Which is part of a good apology? | Showing you understand how it hurt them | Explaining why it was really their fault | Saying it as quickly as possible | Asking for something in return
exercise: How did Joseph's brothers fool their father? | With Joseph's coat dipped in goat's blood | With a letter saying Joseph had run away from home | With a fake grave dug far out in the desert | With a story that a lion had taken him while he slept
exercise: What did the brothers say years later in Egypt? | Surely we are being punished because of our brother | We have never done anything wrong to anyone in our lives | Joseph deserved what happened to him | We forgot all about our brother
exercise: Molly broke her friend's pen and quietly bought a new one. What is missing? | Saying sorry, so her friend knows she understands the hurt | Nothing; a new pen fixes everything, so no words are needed | A second pen to make up for it | A note blaming someone else
exercise: Gran says wee Ciaran says sorry every time and does it again. What does "metanoia" mean? | A change of mind and direction | A very loud apology said in front of everyone | A sorry said three times so it really counts | A kind of punishment given by a priest
exercise: Ethan's mum shouted when tired, and says sorry next morning. Should grown-ups say sorry to children? | Yes; everyone makes mistakes, and it shows respect | No; grown-ups are never wrong, so there's nothing to be sorry for | No; it makes children lose respect | Only if the child says sorry first
exercise: Sofia goes to her first confession. What is the seal of confession? | The priest must never tell anyone what is confessed | A stamp put on a letter to God | A promise never to sin again, written down and signed | A wax seal on the church door
exercise: Nikau snapped the garden sign: "Just say sorry and it's sorted, sweet as." What finishes it? | Fixing the sign as well as saying sorry | Saying sorry three more times, each one louder | Buying the teacher a present to say thank you | Waiting quietly until everyone forgets about it
exercise: Isaac's friend said sorry, but Isaac is still hurt. What is honest? | I'm still upset, give me a bit of time | It's fine, I'm not hurt at all, honestly | I'll never forgive you, ever, whatever you do | Let's just pretend it never happened at all
exercise: Lily drew on the wall at 4, and her brother got the blame. Now she's 9. Is it worth owning up? | Yes; it clears her brother's name | No; it was too long ago to matter | No; little children can't do wrong | Yes, but only if Mum asks first
exercise: What questions does restorative justice ask? | Who was hurt, what do they need, and who should put it right? | Who is to blame, and how long should they be punished? | How much money will it cost the school? | Who saw it, and can we keep it quiet?
exercise: What did Joseph say when he forgave his brothers? | You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good | I will never forgive you as long as I live in this land | You must be my servants for ever | Go home and never come back to Egypt
test: Your mate says, "Sorry's just a word, so it doesn't matter how you say it." What is the best answer? | A real sorry shows you understand the hurt and want to fix it | He's right; any sorry is as good as any other, however you say it | Sorry only counts if a teacher hears it | Saying sorry makes you look weak
test: "Soz babe," texts your cousin after forgetting your birthday. "k," you reply. What would a real apology add? | Naming what happened, understanding the hurt, and offering to make it up | More emojis and a gif | A longer word than "soz" | Nothing; the text is perfect
test: Ava, playing Zacchaeus, says, "He only paid back what he took." What is wrong? | He paid back four times and gave half his things to the poor | He paid back nothing at all | He paid back twice what he took and kept the rest for himself | He only gave money to Jesus
test: Gran in Belfast: "Wee Ciaran says sorry every day, the wee rascal, and does it again." What would help Ciaran? | Making a plan to change, maybe with a grown-up's help | Saying sorry even more times every day until it finally works | Never saying sorry, so it isn't a lie | Blaming his sister instead
test: A cartoon shows a bully who says "sorry" and everyone instantly hugs him, though he does it again. What is missing? | A real change; sorry means trying not to do it again | Nothing; the hug fixes everything and he'll change all by himself | A louder sorry so everyone hears | A present for everyone he hurt
test: Ollie asks, "Do you have to tell the priest everything at confession?" What is the most accurate answer? | Confession is telling God the truth to be forgiven, and the priest keeps it secret for ever | The priest tells your parents everything you say | You only confess the small things, never the big ones | Confession is only for grown-ups who go to prison
test: "Shut up about the sign, I said sorry, didn't I?" snaps Nikau. Why isn't that enough? | The sign is still broken; making amends finishes the sorry | Because he said it too quietly, and a sorry has to be heard | Because the teacher wanted a bigger word | It is enough; nothing else is needed
test: Isaac's mum says, "You must forgive him right now, or you're unforgiving." What does the vicar teach? | Forgiveness can be a journey; being honest about hurt is not unforgiving | Forgiveness must be instant or it doesn't count, so Isaac should hurry up | You only forgive if they give you a present | You never need to forgive anyone
test: What would happen if apologies never came with any change? | Sorry would become an empty word and nobody would trust it | Everyone would be happier and kinder, because nobody would feel guilty | People would stop doing wrong things | Nothing; sorry works on its own anyway
test: Noah asks, "Did Joseph's brothers feel bad for twenty years?" What does the Bible suggest? | Their hidden guilt stayed with them, and forgiveness finally brought peace | They never felt bad at all | They forgot about it the very next day | Joseph never found out what they did
test: Ethan's friend says, "Your mum said sorry to you? That's weird, grown-ups don't do that." What is accurate? | Christians believe everyone needs forgiveness, the parent no less than the child | Grown-ups are never supposed to say sorry, because it makes children lose respect | Only teachers say sorry to children | It's weird, because grown-ups never make mistakes
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the restitution made by Zacchaeus as recorded in Luke 19:1-10? | The restoration of fourfold the amount to any person defrauded, together with the gift of half his possessions to the poor | The restoration of the precise amount defrauded and no more | The payment of a fine to the Roman authorities | The refusal to restore anything, his sins having been forgiven
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of an apology compelled by a teacher and uttered without sincerity. | It lacks the inward sorrow and the intention to make amends which a genuine apology requires | It is too lengthy to be understood by the injured party | It contains excessive detail concerning the offence | It is addressed to an inappropriate person
exam: Select the statement consistent with the meaning of the term "metanoia". | Repentance entails a change of mind and of direction | Repentance entails the repetition of an apology on each occasion | Repentance entails no alteration of conduct whatsoever | Repentance entails the payment of a sum to the church
exam: Ascertain which response is most consistent with this lesson when an apology has been received but the injury is still keenly felt. | The candid acknowledgement that further time is required, forgiveness being a process | The pretence that no injury was ever sustained | The permanent refusal of any forgiveness | The infliction of an equivalent injury upon the offender
